# Citation File Format (CFF) metadata.  GitHub reads this file to
# render the "Cite this repository" panel, and tools such as
# `cffconvert` turn it into BibTeX, EndNote, or APA entries.  Keeping
# it accurate is what makes Imago citable by machine rather than by
# a reader guessing at the right form.
#
# Format reference: https://citation-file-format.github.io
cff-version: 1.2.0

# Shown above the generated citation on the GitHub landing page.
message: >-
  If you use Imago in published work, please cite it using the
  metadata below.

title: Imago
type: software

abstract: >-
  Imago is an all-electron electronic structure code implementing
  the OLCAO (Orthogonalized Linear Combination of Atomic Orbitals)
  method.  It applies to crystals, amorphous solids, nanoparticles,
  molecules, interfaces, and grain boundaries.  Core electrons are
  treated explicitly with no pseudopotentials, periodic boundary
  conditions are used throughout even for non-periodic systems,
  wavefunctions are expressed as linear combinations of atomic
  orbitals, and valence orbitals are orthogonalized to the core to
  shrink the secular equation.

authors:
  - family-names: Rulis
    given-names: Paul
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5783-0206
    affiliation: University of Missouri-Kansas City

repository-code: https://github.com/umkc-cpg/imago
license: ECL-2.0

keywords:
  - electronic structure
  - density functional theory
  - all-electron
  - OLCAO
  - LCAO
  - computational materials science

# Fill these three in at the first tagged release.  `doi` comes from
# Zenodo once the repository is connected and a release is tagged;
# it is the identifier that belongs in a paper's reference list.
#
## version: 1.0.0
## date-released: YYYY-MM-DD
## doi: 10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX
